::ENG4_U06_UA_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.C.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q01
Unit 6 synthesis scenario: A student must combine an oral claim, accessible slides, credited evidence, and a publication format for a community audience. Which option best shows a coherent communication plan? {
~A. Treat the speech, slides, and publication as unrelated products.#Incorrect. This separates connected communication tasks. Unit 6 synthesis requires the oral claim, digital design, evidence, and publication format to work together for one audience purpose.
=B. Use a focused oral claim, accessible slides, credited evidence, and a publication format that fits the audience.#Correct. This answer best applies Unit 6 communication skills with clear reasoning, evidence, audience awareness, and publication readiness.
~C. Add more information to every slide so the presentation seems complete.#Incorrect. More slide text can reduce clarity. Effective digital communication selects the evidence the audience needs and explains extra detail through speaker notes or narration.
~D. Ignore source credits if the final product looks polished.#Incorrect. Polish cannot replace credibility. Publication-ready communication must credit borrowed facts, ideas, and media so the audience can trust the work.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.C.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q02
Presentation excerpt: We need clear evidence, clear reasoning, and clear action. Which rhetorical device is used? {
~A. Unrelated evidence.#Incorrect. The sentence uses a rhetorical structure, not evidence.
~B. Sentence fragment only.#Incorrect. The sentence is complete.
~C. Source citation.#Incorrect. No source is cited.
=D. Repetition and parallel structure.#Correct. Clear is repeated and the phrase pattern is balanced.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.C.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q03
Research brief: A college success office found that 72 percent of surveyed students felt more prepared for interviews after formal presentation practice, and faculty reported that oral explanation helped students clarify claims and evidence. Which option best prepares this research for oral presentation? {
=A. State a focused claim, select credible evidence, explain significance, and acknowledge limits accurately.#Correct. This answer best applies the lesson skill with clear reasoning, appropriate evidence, and audience awareness.
~B. List every research detail without explaining why it matters.#Incorrect. Listing details creates information overload. A strong presentation selects relevant evidence and explains how it supports the claim and audience purpose.
~C. Use only personal opinion because oral presentations should sound casual.#Incorrect. Personal opinion alone does not establish credibility. Oral communication in this unit must use focused claims, evidence, and reasoning.
~D. Ignore limitations so the presentation sounds stronger.#Incorrect. Ignoring limits overstates the evidence. A credible speaker explains what the evidence shows and acknowledges reasonable limits.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.C.02]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q04
Scenario: A student team has copied paragraphs, unrelated photos, an unlabeled chart, and no clear speaker-note plan for explaining evidence about career certification access. Which option best improves digital presentation effectiveness? {
~A. Add unrelated photos so the deck looks more active.#Incorrect. Unrelated images distract from the message. Digital design should use visuals that clarify evidence, claims, or audience understanding.
~B. Keep source paragraphs on slides so the audience can read everything.#Incorrect. Dense paragraphs make slides hard to follow. Effective slides use concise text while the speaker explains evidence and reasoning.
=C. Replace copied paragraphs with concise bullets and speaker notes that explain the evidence.#Correct. This answer best applies the lesson skill with clear reasoning, appropriate evidence, and audience awareness.
~D. Use smaller font to fit more information onto each slide.#Incorrect. Reducing font size hurts readability and access. Revise by selecting key information instead of crowding the slide.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.C.02]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q05
Presentation review: A telehealth presentation includes source citations and a wait-time chart, but it also has dense slide paragraphs, undefined technical terms, low color contrast, and an unlabeled chart for a mixed audience. Which option is the strongest evaluation or revision? {
~A. Judge the presentation only by whether the colors look attractive.#Incorrect. Color is only one design feature. A strong evaluation also checks clarity, credibility, accessibility, evidence, and audience fit.
~B. Keep technical terms undefined because advanced vocabulary always improves credibility.#Incorrect. Undefined technical language can confuse a mixed audience. Academic vocabulary should be precise and explained when the audience needs support.
=C. Define technical terms and reduce dense paragraphs so a mixed audience can follow the message.#Correct. This answer best applies the lesson skill with clear reasoning, evidence, and audience awareness.
~D. Remove source citations because they distract from slide design.#Incorrect. Removing citations weakens credibility. Source credits can be formatted cleanly while still showing responsible evidence use.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.C.03]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q06
Multimedia scenario: A senior project team will publish a multimedia text about youth financial literacy using a written introduction, infographic, narration, source credits, and a shareable digital format. Which option best supports publication-ready multimedia communication? {
=A. Match each media element to a clear purpose and make sure all sources are credited.#Correct. This answer best applies the lesson skill with clear reasoning, evidence, and audience awareness.
~B. Add more media elements even if they repeat the same idea.#Incorrect. Extra media is not automatically stronger. Each mode should add a clear purpose or support audience understanding in a new way.
~C. Publish quickly because multimedia products do not need revision.#Incorrect. Multimedia texts still require revision. Students should check accuracy, access, source credits, and coherence before publishing.
~D. Use borrowed visuals without credit if they make the product more attractive.#Incorrect. Borrowed visuals require credit. Ethical publication depends on acknowledging outside media and respecting source use.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.C.03]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q07
Unit 6 publication checklist: A final product includes narration, slides, an infographic, a research summary, and source credits. Which option best checks whether the pieces work together for the audience? {
~A. Add animations before checking source credits or audience access.#Incorrect. Animations are secondary to credibility and accessibility. A presentation should first make sources, evidence, and audience access secure.
~B. Publish as soon as the product contains several media parts.#Incorrect. Having several media parts is not enough. Publication readiness requires each part to support the purpose, audience, and source responsibilities.
~C. Remove evidence so the product feels shorter and more casual.#Incorrect. Shorter is not always stronger. Unit 6 communication still needs credible evidence, even when the style is concise.
=D. Verify that narration, slides, infographic, research summary, and source credits work together for the audience.#Correct. This answer best applies Unit 6 communication skills with clear reasoning, evidence, audience awareness, and publication readiness.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.C.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q08
Oral presentation planning: A speaker needs listeners to understand the point, support, reasoning, and ending. Which order best supports a focused oral presentation? {
~A. Evidence, unrelated detail, focus, conclusion.#Incorrect. The listener needs the focus before evidence.
=B. Focus, evidence, explanation, conclusion.#Correct. This order helps listeners follow the point and support.
~C. Conclusion, evidence, unrelated detail, focus.#Incorrect. This order is confusing.
~D. Unrelated detail, evidence, conclusion, focus.#Incorrect. This does not support clear oral communication.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.C.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q09
Presentation excerpt: We need clear evidence, clear reasoning, and clear action. Which device is used? {
~A. Unrelated evidence.#Incorrect. The sentence uses a rhetorical structure, not evidence.
~B. Sentence fragment only.#Incorrect. The sentence is complete.
=C. Repetition and parallel structure.#Correct. Clear is repeated and the phrase pattern is balanced.
~D. Source citation.#Incorrect. No source is cited.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.C.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q10
Research brief: A college success office found that 72 percent of surveyed students felt more prepared for interviews after formal presentation practice, and faculty reported that oral explanation helped students clarify claims and evidence. Which option best prepares this research for oral presentation? {
=A. State a focused claim, select credible evidence, explain significance, and acknowledge limits accurately.#Correct. This answer best applies the lesson skill with clear reasoning, appropriate evidence, and audience awareness.
~B. List every research detail without explaining why it matters.#Incorrect. Listing details creates information overload. A strong presentation selects relevant evidence and explains how it supports the claim and audience purpose.
~C. Use only personal opinion because oral presentations should sound casual.#Incorrect. Personal opinion alone does not establish credibility. Oral communication in this unit must use focused claims, evidence, and reasoning.
~D. Ignore limitations so the presentation sounds stronger.#Incorrect. Ignoring limits overstates the evidence. A credible speaker explains what the evidence shows and acknowledges reasonable limits.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q11::[MLA.ENG4.C.02]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q11
Scenario: A student team has copied paragraphs, unrelated photos, an unlabeled chart, and no clear speaker-note plan for explaining evidence about career certification access. Which option best improves digital presentation effectiveness? {
~A. Add unrelated photos so the deck looks more active.#Incorrect. Unrelated images distract from the message. Digital design should use visuals that clarify evidence, claims, or audience understanding.
~B. Keep source paragraphs on slides so the audience can read everything.#Incorrect. Dense paragraphs make slides hard to follow. Effective slides use concise text while the speaker explains evidence and reasoning.
~C. Use smaller font to fit more information onto each slide.#Incorrect. Reducing font size hurts readability and access. Revise by selecting key information instead of crowding the slide.
=D. Replace copied paragraphs with concise bullets and speaker notes that explain the evidence.#Correct. This answer best applies the lesson skill with clear reasoning, appropriate evidence, and audience awareness.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q12::[MLA.ENG4.C.02]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q12
Presentation review: A telehealth presentation includes source citations and a wait-time chart, but it also has dense slide paragraphs, undefined technical terms, low color contrast, and an unlabeled chart for a mixed audience. Which option is the strongest evaluation or revision? {
~A. Judge the presentation only by whether the colors look attractive.#Incorrect. Color is only one design feature. A strong evaluation also checks clarity, credibility, accessibility, evidence, and audience fit.
=B. Define technical terms and reduce dense paragraphs so a mixed audience can follow the message.#Correct. This answer best applies the lesson skill with clear reasoning, evidence, and audience awareness.
~C. Keep technical terms undefined because advanced vocabulary always improves credibility.#Incorrect. Undefined technical language can confuse a mixed audience. Academic vocabulary should be precise and explained when the audience needs support.
~D. Remove source citations because they distract from slide design.#Incorrect. Removing citations weakens credibility. Source credits can be formatted cleanly while still showing responsible evidence use.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q13::[MLA.ENG4.C.03]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q13
Multimedia scenario: A senior project team will publish a multimedia text about youth financial literacy using a written introduction, infographic, narration, source credits, and a shareable digital format. Which option best supports publication-ready multimedia communication? {
~A. Add more media elements even if they repeat the same idea.#Incorrect. Extra media is not automatically stronger. Each mode should add a clear purpose or support audience understanding in a new way.
=B. Match each media element to a clear purpose and make sure all sources are credited.#Correct. This answer best applies the lesson skill with clear reasoning, evidence, and audience awareness.
~C. Publish quickly because multimedia products do not need revision.#Incorrect. Multimedia texts still require revision. Students should check accuracy, access, source credits, and coherence before publishing.
~D. Use borrowed visuals without credit if they make the product more attractive.#Incorrect. Borrowed visuals require credit. Ethical publication depends on acknowledging outside media and respecting source use.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q14::[MLA.ENG4.C.03]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q14
Publication scenario: A senior team has a one-minute narration, three slides, an infographic, a research summary, and source credits for a public-service announcement about postsecondary training. Which option best supports publication-ready multimedia communication? {
~A. Add animations before checking source credits or audience access.#Incorrect. Animations are secondary to credibility and accessibility. A presentation should first make sources, evidence, and audience access secure.
~B. Publish as soon as the product contains several media parts.#Incorrect. Having several media parts is not enough. Publication readiness requires each part to support the purpose, audience, and source responsibilities.
=C. Verify that narration, slides, infographic, research summary, and source credits work together for the audience.#Correct. This answer best applies Unit 6 communication skills with clear reasoning, evidence, audience awareness, and publication readiness.
~D. Remove evidence so the product feels shorter and more casual.#Incorrect. Shorter is not always stronger. Unit 6 communication still needs credible evidence, even when the style is concise.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q15::[MLA.ENG4.C.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q15
Which oral response order is strongest for listeners? {
=A. Focus, evidence, explanation, conclusion.#Correct. This order helps listeners follow the point and support.
~B. Evidence, unrelated detail, focus, conclusion.#Incorrect. The listener needs the focus before evidence.
~C. Conclusion, evidence, unrelated detail, focus.#Incorrect. This order is confusing.
~D. Unrelated detail, evidence, conclusion, focus.#Incorrect. This does not support clear oral communication.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q16::[MLA.ENG4.R.12]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q16
Presentation excerpt: We need clear evidence, clear reasoning, and clear action. Why is the repeated word clear effective in the excerpt? {
~A. It distracts from the point.#Incorrect. The repetition supports the point.
~B. It replaces all evidence.#Incorrect. Rhetoric should not replace evidence.
~C. It makes the claim unrelated.#Incorrect. The repetition reinforces the claim.
=D. It emphasizes the qualities the speaker wants the audience to remember.#Correct. Repetition makes the key idea memorable.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q17::[MLA.ENG4.R.10]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q17
Source note: Workers with unreliable transportation are more likely to miss scheduled shifts. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Workers with reliable transportation miss more shifts.#Incorrect. This reverses the meaning.
~B. Transportation has no effect on work schedules.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source note.
=C. Unreliable transportation can make workers miss scheduled shifts.#Correct. This preserves the meaning.
~D. Workers should avoid all transportation.#Incorrect. This adds an unsupported idea.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q18::[MLA.ENG4.V.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q18
Which sentence uses academic vocabulary effectively in an oral response? {
=A. The evidence is credible because it comes from a local workforce report.#Correct. Credible is used accurately.
~B. The thing is good because stuff happens.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~C. Evidence is a word in the presentation.#Incorrect. This does not use academic vocabulary meaningfully.
~D. The claim is claimy and has evidence things.#Incorrect. This is not academic language.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q19::[MLA.ENG4.L.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q19
Which spoken sentence is clearest and most conventional? {
~A. Evidence supports claim because report.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
=B. The evidence supports the claim because it comes from a credible report.#Correct. This is clear and conventional.
~C. The evidence supports the claim it comes from report.#Incorrect. This is a run-on sentence.
~D. Because credible report evidence.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q20::[MLA.ENG4.C.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q20
Which statement best explains why focus matters in oral communication? {
~A. Focus matters only in writing, not speaking.#Incorrect. Focus is essential in oral communication.
~B. Focus means adding as many topics as possible.#Incorrect. Focus narrows the message.
~C. Focus replaces evidence.#Incorrect. Focus and evidence work together.
=D. A clear focus helps listeners understand how each piece of evidence supports the main point.#Correct. Focus guides listener understanding.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q21::[MLA.ENG4.C.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q21
Which opening is most focused for an oral presentation? {
=A. Reliable transportation supports employment stability by reducing missed shifts.#Correct. This gives a clear and specific focus.
~B. I will talk about transportation and maybe jobs.#Incorrect. This is unfocused.
~C. Transportation is a thing people use.#Incorrect. This is too general.
~D. There are many topics to discuss.#Incorrect. This does not establish a focus.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q22::[MLA.ENG4.R.10]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q22
Source note: A city report links unreliable transit to increased worker absences. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Reliable transit causes worker absences.#Incorrect. This reverses the source meaning.
~B. A city report says absences never relate to transit.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source note.
=C. A city report connects unreliable transit with more worker absences.#Correct. This preserves the meaning.
~D. Workers are absent because reports exist.#Incorrect. This is illogical.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q23::[MLA.ENG4.R.08]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q23
Which use of rhetoric is weakest? {
~A. A rhetorical question after evidence has been explained.#Incorrect. That can be effective.
~B. Parallel structure that organizes three reasons.#Incorrect. That can strengthen organization.
~C. Repetition that emphasizes a proven pattern.#Incorrect. That can support evidence.
=D. A dramatic claim with no evidence or reasoning.#Correct. Rhetoric without evidence weakens argument quality.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q24::[MLA.ENG4.V.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q24
Which word best describes evidence from a trustworthy public report? {
~A. decorative.#Incorrect. Decorative does not describe trustworthiness.
=B. credible.#Correct. Credible means trustworthy or believable.
~C. random.#Incorrect. Random does not show reliability.
~D. unrelated.#Incorrect. Unrelated does not describe source quality.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q25::[MLA.ENG4.L.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q25
Which sentence uses standard English? {
=A. The evidence is credible because it comes from a public report.#Correct. This is clear and complete.
~B. The evidence credible because report.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
~C. The evidence is credible it comes from a public report.#Incorrect. This is a run-on sentence.
~D. Because evidence credible public report.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q26::[MLA.ENG4.C.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q26
What should come immediately after a focused claim in a short oral response? {
~A. An unrelated personal story.#Incorrect. This distracts from the focus.
~B. A conclusion with no support.#Incorrect. The listener needs evidence first.
~C. A new topic.#Incorrect. This weakens organization.
=D. Credible evidence that supports the claim.#Correct. Evidence should support the focus.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q27::[MLA.ENG4.R.10]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q27
Source note: Workers with unreliable transportation may miss scheduled shifts even when they want to keep steady employment. Which paraphrase best preserves the source note? {
~A. Workers with reliable transportation miss more shifts.#Incorrect. This reverses the meaning.
~B. Transportation has no effect on work schedules.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source note.
=C. Unreliable transportation can make workers miss scheduled shifts.#Correct. This preserves the meaning.
~D. Workers should avoid all transportation.#Incorrect. This adds an unsupported idea.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q28::[MLA.ENG4.V.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q28
Presentation sentence: The evidence is credible because it comes from a local workforce report. Which option uses academic vocabulary most effectively? {
~A. The thing is good because stuff happens.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
=B. The evidence is credible because it comes from a local workforce report.#Correct. Credible is used accurately.
~C. Evidence is a word in the presentation.#Incorrect. This does not use academic vocabulary meaningfully.
~D. The claim is claimy and has evidence things.#Incorrect. This is not academic language.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q29::[MLA.ENG4.R.10]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q29
Source note: Clear emergency communication helps residents make informed safety decisions. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
=A. Clear emergency messages can help residents make informed safety choices.#Correct. This preserves the meaning.
~B. Emergency communication prevents all danger.#Incorrect. This overstates the source.
~C. Residents never use emergency communication.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source.
~D. Safety decisions are unrelated to communication.#Incorrect. The source connects them.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q30::[MLA.ENG4.R.12]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q30
Presentation excerpt: We need clear evidence, clear reasoning, and clear action. How does the repetition affect the presentation? {
~A. It distracts from the point.#Incorrect. The repetition supports the point.
~B. It replaces all evidence.#Incorrect. Rhetoric should not replace evidence.
=C. It emphasizes the qualities the speaker wants the audience to remember.#Correct. Repetition makes the key idea memorable.
~D. It makes the claim unrelated.#Incorrect. The repetition reinforces the claim.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q31::[MLA.ENG4.C.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q31
Which transition best introduces evidence in an oral response? {
~A. In conclusion,#Incorrect. This signals ending, not evidence.
=B. According to the report,#Correct. This introduces source-based evidence.
~C. On a totally different topic,#Incorrect. This signals a shift away from the focus.
~D. Anyway,#Incorrect. This is informal and unfocused.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q32::[MLA.ENG4.V.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q32
Which sentence uses academic vocabulary precisely? {
~A. The evidence thing is good.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. The claim has stuff.#Incorrect. This is informal.
~C. The report is report-like.#Incorrect. This is not precise.
=D. The evidence supports the claim by showing a measurable pattern.#Correct. Supports, claim, and measurable are used precisely.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q33::[MLA.ENG4.L.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q33
Which revision fixes the sentence? Draft: The focus is clear it helps listeners follow the evidence. {
~A. The focus is clear it helps listeners follow the evidence.#Incorrect. This is a run-on.
~B. Focus clear helps listeners evidence.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
~C. Because the focus clear.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
=D. The focus is clear, and it helps listeners follow the evidence.#Correct. This correctly joins two complete ideas.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q34::[MLA.ENG4.C.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q34
Which response best explains evidence after presenting it? {
~A. That is evidence.#Incorrect. This labels but does not explain.
~B. Moving to a new unrelated topic.#Incorrect. This does not explain evidence.
=C. This matters because missed shifts can reduce income and job stability.#Correct. This explains why the evidence matters.
~D. Evidence is evidence.#Incorrect. This is circular.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q35::[MLA.ENG4.C.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q35
Which oral response problem is most serious? {
=A. The speaker lists evidence but never states a focus.#Correct. Without focus, listeners cannot organize the evidence.
~B. The speaker uses one clear transition.#Incorrect. A transition helps.
~C. The speaker explains why evidence matters.#Incorrect. Explanation is useful.
~D. The speaker ends with a conclusion.#Incorrect. A conclusion can help.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q36::[MLA.ENG4.L.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q36
Revision task: Choose the sentence that uses standard English to connect evidence and claim clearly. {
~A. Evidence supports claim because report.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
=B. The evidence supports the claim because it comes from a credible report.#Correct. This is clear and conventional.
~C. The evidence supports the claim it comes from report.#Incorrect. This is a run-on sentence.
~D. Because credible report evidence.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q37::[MLA.ENG4.V.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q37
Which explanation uses academic vocabulary precisely? {
=A. The speaker's parallel structure emphasizes three related claims.#Correct. This uses parallel structure and emphasizes accurately.
~B. The speaker says words in a speech.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~C. The speech is speechy and good.#Incorrect. This is not academic language.
~D. The device is a thing.#Incorrect. This is vague.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q38::[MLA.ENG4.R.10]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q38
Source note: The study found that tutoring increased course completion rates. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Tutoring lowered course completion rates.#Incorrect. This reverses the meaning.
~B. The study ignored tutoring.#Incorrect. Tutoring is the focus.
~C. Course completion was unrelated to support.#Incorrect. The source note connects them.
=D. The study found that students completed courses more often when tutoring was available.#Correct. This preserves the meaning.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q39::[MLA.ENG4.V.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q39
What does concise mean in oral communication? {
~A. confusing and long.#Incorrect. Concise means brief and clear.
~B. unrelated to the topic.#Incorrect. Concise communication stays focused.
=C. brief, clear, and focused.#Correct. Concise speech avoids unnecessary wording.
~D. informal and vague.#Incorrect. Concise does not mean informal.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q40::[MLA.ENG4.L.01]
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q40
Which sentence is ready for a formal oral presentation? {
~A. The report says stuff about transit.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=B. The report explains how transit reliability affects worker attendance.#Correct. This is clear and formal.
~C. Transit stuff is in the report.#Incorrect. This is informal.
~D. Report transit worker attendance affects.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
}
